      "mergeID": null,
      "parentID": null,
      "pinned": false,
      "postDeleted": false,
      "postID": "60614cda583e420790a0a6fa",
      "private": false,
      "reactions": {
        "like": 1
      },
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "I'm working on a portals prefab, and having the player's avatar IPD is necessary for getting the portals to look right. Currently the best way I've found is Merlin's PlayspaceTracking prefab (https://github.com/MerlinVR/PlayspaceTracking) but it jitters when switching avatars and has issues with floating point precision in large worlds. Having the player camera's scale/stereoSeparation value exposed in Udon would be very helpful!\n\nEDIT: The comment below by UdonEats does still work as of 1/2/2023! To elaborate, all that's strictly necessary is an *active* GameObject with an Audio Listener -- its scale will get set to 1/trackingScale, for mysterious reasons. However, ClientSim deletes all Audio Listener components (on active GameObjects only) in the scene when entering play mode. Adding the Audio Distortion Filter, which depends on the Audio Listener, prevents it from getting deleted. It does cause an error to be emitted in the console every time you enter playmode, though. Luckily, it seems that real in-game VRC doesn't delete Audio Listeners, and also ClientSim only checks active GameObjects, so having an inactive-by-default GameObject with an Audio Listener should work fine! It might still be good measure to add the Audio Distortion Filter anyway, just in case! Anyway, overall this method certainly seems like it could break at any time, but cool while it works! Thanks UdonEats!",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    },
